{"results":[{"url":"https://www.pa.gov/agencies/vote/elections/fact-checking-pa-related-election-claims","final_url":"https://www.pa.gov/agencies/vote/elections/fact-checking-pa-related-election-claims","title":"Fact-Checking PA-related Election Claims","description":"Fact-Checking PA-related Election Claims","language":"en","text":"# Fact-Checking Pennsylvania-Related Election Claims\n\nWe are committed to providing accurate, trustworthy information about election administration in Pennsylvania and to combatting damaging misinformation and disinformation about our elections.\n\n## Voting by mail is safe and secure, and no evidence exists of widespread mail voting fraud in Pennsylvania.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nMail ballot fraud has been proven to be exceptionally rare. There is no evidence to support claims of systemic voter fraud and those claims have been consistently rejected by judges, government agencies, and election experts across the political spectrum.\n\n### \n\nMail voting in Pennsylvania has multiple layers of security that involve registered voters having to provide appropriate ID before their mail ballot can be counted and that prevent any voter from casting more than one ballot in any given election.\n\nPennsylvanians have been voting by mail since the Civil War, and the process has a long history of being safe and secure.\n\n## All eligible Pennsylvanians can securely vote by mail. The United States Supreme Court has decided that a USPS rule attempting to unlawfully regulate voting by mail will remain blocked. Counties are preparing to provide mail ballots as usual.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nVoters in Pennsylvania should feel confident that they can vote safely and securely by mail, and that their counties will continue to follow existing processes which ensure that only eligible Pennsylvania voters can cast ballots. Voters who cast their ballot by mail or over the counter at their county elections office can still do so safely and securely. As always, mail ballots should be returned as early as possible after voters have received them. Ballots received after 8 p.m. on Election Day cannot be counted.\n\nUSPS’s recent effort to change mail balloting rules has been stopped by the United States Supreme Court, and the law in Pennsylvania remains unchanged for the November 2026 election. Counties continue to prepare for a free, fair, safe, and secure election, which includes voting by mail.  Pennsylvania will always defend the right of every eligible voter to cast their ballot by any lawful means, including in person, over the counter, or by mail.\n\nEvery mail ballot will be counted according to the laws governing Pennsylvania’s secure methods for voting by mail.  Every mail ballot applicant’s ID is verified before their mail ballot can be counted, and counties routinely compare mail ballots to reliable data to ensure they do not count the ballot of anyone who has passed away. Every mail ballot envelope has a unique barcode that ties the mail ballot package to an eligible, registered voter.\n\n### \n\nPennsylvanians have been voting by mail since the Civil War, and the process has a long history of being safe and secure. The Shapiro Administration and the Department of State will continue to fight to ensure eligible Pennsylvania voters can access voting by mail in spite of unprecedented federal overreach into our state’s elections.\n\n Voters may have seen reporting about a new rule by the United States Postal Service that would have affected mail ballots.  The Shapiro Administration, along with 23 other states and the District of Columbia, filed a lawsuit and successfully obtained an injunction blocking USPS’ rule from taking effect.  The United States Supreme Court denied a request to stop that injunction. The USPS rule will therefore not be in effect for the November 2026 Election. Preparations for the November 2026 election are proceeding as usual under state and federal law.\n\nVoters should have confidence that Pennsylvania officials will protect their right to vote, that the Department of State will continue to provide timely updates on the rules for voting in Pennsylvania, that counties will continue to administer elections under existing law, and that voters will be able to safely and securely cast their ballots by mail.\n\nFederal interference does not make our elections more secure.  Mail voting in Pennsylvania has multiple layers of security that involve registered voters having to provide appropriate ID before their mail ballot can be counted and that prevent any voter from casting more than one ballot in any given election. Counties routinely compare mail ballots to reliable data to ensure they do not count the ballot of anyone who has passed away, and every mail ballot envelope has a unique barcode that ties the mail ballot package to an eligible, registered voter.\n\n## State law, not federal law, sets the deadline for receipt of mail ballots in elections. In Pennsylvania, nearly all mail ballots must be received by 8p.m. on Election Day. The only exception is for some military and overseas voters.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nCounty boards of elections must receive a voter’s completed mail ballot by 8 p.m. on Election Day for it to be counted. The deadline applies to ballots returned by mail or in person to a county office or drop box. There is no grace period. Ballots received after that time, even if they are postmarked by Election Day, do not count. The only exception is for military and overseas voters, whose ballots must be counted if they are received within seven days of the election and are submitted by the voter for mailing or other authorized means of delivery on the day before Election Day. Learn more about mail in voting in Pennsylvania.\n\n### \n\nThe United States Supreme Court recently issued a decision confirming that state law sets the deadline for receiving mail ballots, even in federal elections. That means in some states mail ballots will be counted if they arrive after election day, **but that is not the rule in Pennsylvania.**\n\nPennsylvania law requires that all mail ballots must be received by 8:00 p.m. on Election Day to be counted, whether returned by mail or in person. This means that, if you mail your ballot back, you must allow enough time for it to get to the board of elections by 8:00 p.m. on Election Day. If you return your ballot in person—either over the counter at an election office or in a county drop box—you must do so by 8:00 p.m. on Election Day. State law does not provide for any grace period, even if the ballot is postmarked prior to Election Day.  Pennsylvania state law has a limited exception for military and overseas voters, whose ballots are counted as long as they were mailed by the voter on the day before the election.\n\n## Requesting and returning a mail ballot will make a voter ineligible to vote by regular ballot at the polls. Mail ballots are not “emergency backup ballots,” because they are the first ballot counted if they are returned by election day.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nRequesting and returning a mail ballot will make a voter ineligible to vote by regular ballot at the polls.  If an emergency occurs on Election Day that prevents a voter from reporting to the polling place, the voter can apply for an emergency absentee ballot, which can be picked up by the voter or by an authorized representative.\n\nMail ballots are available to all eligible Pennsylvanians and should be used when the voter does not plan to vote in person at their polling place on Election Day. They are not “emergency backup ballots.” If a voter plans to vote in person, they should not request a mail ballot. Returning a mail ballot prior to Election Day will create a record in the system that prevents a voter from casting a regular ballot on Election Day, as each voter may only vote once.  Voters who request a mail ballot and do not complete it will be required to vote provisionally if they report to their polling place on Election Day unless they bring their mail ballot and return envelope with them to be spoiled by election workers at the polling place.  Emergency absentee ballots do exist, but are distinct.  Emergency absentee ballots can be used by a person who did not apply for a mail ballot by the standard application deadline (seven days before Election Day) if an emergency arises after that deadline but before Election Day that prevents the person  from getting to the polls. The applicant must  attest to the reason why the application is an emergency.\n\n### \n\nMail ballots allow a voter to receive their ballot prior to Election Day, fill it out, and return it by mail, directly to their county elections office, or at another site authorized by the county.  All registered Pennsylvania voters have the option to vote by mail.  For the 2026 General Election, the deadline to apply for a mail ballot is 5 pm on October 27. The county elections office must receive the completed ballot by 8 pm on November 3 (Election Day).  Voters may apply for a mail ballot online or via paper application submitted to their county elections office by mail or in person.  Some counties have set up satellite county offices that provide voters with more options to apply for, receive, and vote their mail ballot.   Mail ballots should be returned at the voter’s earliest possible convenience to avoid delivery after Election Day, at which point the ballot cannot be counted.\n\nIf a voter requests a mail ballot and returns it, they cannot vote a regular ballot at the polls on Election Day.  If the voter has been sent a mail ballot and wishes to vote in person at the polls on Election Day they will have to vote provisionally unless they bring the ballot and the return envelope to the polls to be spoiled.  In no event may a voter cast more than one ballot, and safeguards in the Pennsylvania system will prevent them from doing so.  If a voter plans to vote in person, using a regular mail ballot as a “backup” may result in having to vote provisionally, and the provisional ballot will not be counted if the mail ballot is counted by the county.  Requesting a mail ballot with an intention to vote at the polls creates additional work for county poll workers which will ultimately cause longer lines at the polls.  Any person applying for a mail ballot at least seven days before Election Day will be issued a standard mail-in or absentee ballot.\n\nAn emergency absentee ballot can only be requested in certain limited circumstances involving last-minute emergencies that occur after the mail ballot application deadline (these can include an unexpected illness or a last-minute absence from a voter's municipality).  To obtain an emergency ballot, a voter must request one between 5 pm on the Tuesday before the election (the mail ballot deadline) and 8 pm on Election Day.  The application forms, including one for an authorized representative to pick up an emergency ballot, can be found on the Pennsylvania Department of State’s website.  This type of ballot exists to ensure that an unexpected occurrence shortly before or on Election Day does not prevent a voter from casting their ballot.\n\n## **County election officials, not the Department of State, do the important work of keeping voter rolls up to date through a process known as voter list maintenance, and much of this work is done annually, typically between May and July. Their work is carefully regulated by federal and state law, which instructs on how voter records are cancelled and moved from Active to Inactive status. In Pennsylvania, Inactive voters are still eligible to vote by mail and at their polling place if they meet the state ID requirements.**\n\n**The short answer**\n\nPennsylvania’s 67 county elections offices maintain Pennsylvania’s voter registration rolls through a centralized database known as the SURE system (Statewide Uniform Registry of Electors). County officials follow rigorous procedures dictated by federal and state law, and they use multiple reliable sources to ensure that voter lists are as accurate as possible while protecting voters from being wrongfully removed. They conduct these activities on an annual basis, and a full description of this process, and data on the number of voters affected, are published by the Department every year in an Annual Report.\n\nMuch of the work done by counties is performed between May and July each year, and involves the careful cancellation of records, as well as moving voter records from “Active” status to “Inactive” status. Voters may be placed on inactive status when they are identified as having submitted a USPS mail forwarding request, registered in another state, or they have not taken any action on their voter record for a five-year period. They may also be placed on inactive status when they do not respond to an initial notice of receipt of a change of address mailing or when a mailing to their registered address is returned as undeliverable.\n\nThe process of moving voters from active to inactive status is part of the routine list maintenance activity regulated by state and federal law, and it does not remove them from the voter rolls. Inactive voters can vote by mail or at their polling place if they provide the proper ID. An Inactive voter who does not update their record or vote will generally have their voter record cancelled after the passage of two general federal elections.\n\n### \n\nIt is important to remember that a voter list will never be 100% accurate at a given moment in time. That is because voters die, move, or update their registrations every day. Both federal and state law recognize this and set up a careful balance so that, on the one hand, voters who have died or moved can be removed from the voter rolls but, on the other hand, voters will not be wrongfully disenfranchised through improper cancellation of their registration.\n\nCounty elections officials regularly review data sources such as postal service change of address notices, state death records, and obituaries to check for voters who have moved or died. The information they look for, and how it can be used, is also regulated by state and federal law.\n\nFor example, county elections officials receive regular death notices from the state Department of Health. Pennsylvania’s membership in the Electronic Registration Information Center (ERIC) ensures the Department of State is notified when a voter moves to a new state so the Department can notify the voter’s prior home county for further action.\n\nSpecifically, when voters appear on postal change of address notices, counties are legally required to contact them to verify whether their registration should be cancelled, updated, or kept the same. Many people may temporarily change their address – for reasons such as going away to college or working a temporary job – but intend to maintain their registration in Pennsylvania. The processes in place make sure these voters can stay registered at their home if they choose.\n\nAlso, when people move from one state to another, they often register to vote in their new state but don’t think to cancel their registration in their former state. Again, the procedures in place ensure the outdated registration is ultimately cancelled.\n\nPennsylvania’s 67 counties undertake their list maintenance activities on an annual basis, often between May and July. It involves using the data sources described above to cancel voter records, or move a voter record from active to inactive status. Moving a voter record to Inactive status does not remove them from the voter rolls. Inactive voters can vote by mail or at their polling place if they provide the proper ID. An Inactive voter who does not update their record or vote will generally have their voter record cancelled after the passage of two general federal elections.\n\nIn Pennsylvania, every voter must take steps to verify their identity before they cast a ballot, including providing proper identification every time they register to vote, vote by mail, or vote at a new polling place. All evidence has shown that noncitizen voting is extremely rare across the country, including in Pennsylvania.\n\nTo learn more about voter list maintenance in Pennsylvania – including exploring historic data on voter registration cancellations – visit our Election Reports page and scroll down to “Annual Reports on Voter Registration,” where you can view reports back to 1995.\n\n## Noncitizen voting is illegal and carries significant penalties.\n\n**The short answer**\n\n**It is illegal for noncitizens to vote in state or federal elections.** There are significant consequences if a noncitizen registers to vote and/or votes in a state or federal election. Under state law, individuals must swear under criminal penalty that they are a U.S. citizen when they sign the voter registration application. This includes acknowledging that if the information is untrue that they can be convicted of perjury, fined up to $15,000, jailed, and deported.\n\n### \n\nEven though instances are extremely rare, DOS takes very seriously any threats to election security.\n\n**In Pennsylvania, there are several steps to verify your identity in order to cast a ballot:**\n\n* **Voters are asked to provide a PA driver’s license number, PA state ID number, or the last four digits of their Social Security number when they register to vote and every time they vote by mail.**\n* **In-person voters must show appropriate ID when they vote at a new polling place for the first time.**\n* **Under state law, every voter registration applicant must swear they are a U.S. citizen and that they understand if they submit false information, they could be subjected to incarceration, fines, and/or deportation.**\n\nMultiple reputable nonpartisan groups, including the Bipartisan Policy Center and the Brennan Center for Justice, have repeatedly found that noncitizen voting is extremely rare in every state.\n\nVoter rolls are maintained at the county level. DOS operates a PA Voter Registration website for voters who want to register online, and those records are passed along to the appropriate county and county officials to review all registration applications.\n\n## Pennsylvania’s automatic voter registration (AVR) only permits Pennsylvanians with documents proving their voting eligibility, including U.S. citizenship, to submit a voter registration application through PennDOT. All eligible applicants are also given the opportunity to opt out.  Noncitizens are prohibited from accessing the AVR process, and no Pennsylvania resident is ever registered to vote without their consent.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nPennsylvania’s AVR process has built-in protections that enable only U.S. citizens to apply to register to vote.\n\nTo be eligible to register to vote in Pennsylvania, applicants must be:\n\n* a U.S. citizen for at least 30 days,\n\n* a resident of Pennsylvania for at least 30 days, and\n\n* at least 18 years old on or before the date of the next election.\n\nWhen Pennsylvania residents go to Department of Transportation (PennDOT) centers to obtain a new or renewed driver’s license or state ID, only those residents who meet all three of the above criteria as documented in PennDOT’s records are presented with the automatic voter registration (AVR) screens. Since Pennsylvania only issues PennDOT IDs to citizens and immigrants who can show lawful status, all PennDOT customers present paperwork showing that they are a citizen or a noncitizen here legally. If a customer shows paperwork establishing that they are a citizen, they pass through to the AVR screens. If they show paperwork establishing that they are a lawfully present non-citizen, PennDOT applies a code to their ID record. Having the noncitizen code on their record prevents them from ever seeing the AVR screens.  Eligible applicants who are shown the AVR screens must additionally affirm that they are U.S. citizens on separate screens and are always presented with an opportunity to opt out of the AVR process.\n\n### \n\nPennsylvania’s AVR process has built-in protections that enable only U.S. citizens to apply to register to vote.\n\nWhen Pennsylvanians visit a PennDOT driver or photo license center to apply for a driver’s license or other state ID, they must provide documentation that proves their age, residency, and citizenship – all requirements needed to determine if a person is eligible to register to vote in Pennsylvania. Since Pennsylvania only issues PennDOT IDs to citizens and immigrants who can show lawful status, all PennDOT customers present paperwork showing that they are either a citizen or a noncitizen whose presence in the U.S. is lawful. If an individual shows records establishing lawful presence, but not citizenship, an indicator is placed on that individual’s PennDOT ID record.\n\nBased on PennDOT’s records, if the applicant is at least 18 years old on or before the date of the next election, has been a resident of Pennsylvania for at least 30 days, and has been a U.S. citizen for at least 30 days, they are automatically presented with the AVR screens. Individuals with an indicator on their record that they are a lawfully present noncitizen are not shown the AVR screens. The AVR screens also provide three additional occasions to confirm or deny US citizenship and an opportunity to decline to apply for voter registration.\n\nOnce an applicant goes through the AVR process, their application is sent to the elections office in the county where they live, and that county elections office once again reviews the applicant’s eligibility before the applicant is officially registered to vote.\n\nUnder state law, an applicant swears they are a U.S. citizen when they sign the voter registration application and acknowledge they understand that, if the information is not true, they can be convicted of perjury, fined up to $15,000, jailed, and deported.\n\nThe transmission of voter applications from PennDOT to county election offices is required by  National Voter Registration Act, also known as the motor voter law and Pennsylvania law. Voter rolls are maintained at the county level.\n\n## Inclusion on a nonjuror list does not mean someone is registered to vote. Any resident of Pennsylvania—including a noncitizen—who pays taxes, drives a car, or interacts with the Commonwealth in a number of different ways, may be included on the initial county juror list.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nThe Administrative Office of the Pennsylvania Courts (AOPC) compiles a list of potential jurors from comprehensive lists provided by Pennsylvania’s Departments of Revenue, Transportation, Human Services, and State, and it provides those lists to county courts. Under Pennsylvania law, the comprehensive lists from these agencies must include noncitizens who pay taxes, have a PennDOT ID, or receive state assistance from DHS.  The AOPC removes personal identifying information and provides counties with the composite list so the counties can use it to select jurors. When noncitizens on the list are summoned for jury duty, they indicate they are not eligible and are placed on nonjuror lists.\n\n### \n\nAs of 2008, all counties in Pennsylvania can receive juror lists from the Administrative Office of Pennsylvania Courts (AOPC).  The AOPC develops its county lists of potential jurors from multiple sources, including the Department of Revenue, the Department of Transportation, the Department of Human Services, and the Department of State. Under 42 Pa.C.S.A. § 4521.1, these agencies must provide, among other things, “every individual resident in this Commonwealth who has been issued a driver's license” and “every individual resident who files a return for the payment of taxes imposed by the Commonwealth.” Pennsylvania residents who are not citizens but do have legal status to be in the U.S. may obtain driver’s licenses and must pay taxes, so they must be included on the initial juror lists.\n\nHowever, noncitizens are not permitted to serve on juries, and when noncitizens on the AOPC list are called by their counties, they indicate that they are not eligible and are placed on a so-called “nonjuror list.”  The presence of any individual on the juror list, or on subsequent nonjuror lists, does not indicate whether they are registered to vote.\n\nAlso, no confidential information about individuals on the juror list is passed on to the counties, so, the lists themselves do not contain the personally identifiable information needed to match with individuals on the voter rolls.  Nonjuror lists are not a useful tool to verify the accuracy of voter rolls.  Instead, analyses of voter lists that have relied on faulty methodologies such as comparisons to nonjuror lists can lead to inaccurate results and false claims about the citizenship status of eligible voters.\n\nPennsylvania follows all state and federal laws when it comes to our elections to ensure that our voter rolls are properly maintained and updated. In Pennsylvania, every voter must take steps to verify their identity before they cast a ballot, including providing proper identification every time they register to vote, vote by mail, or vote at a new polling place. All evidence has shown that noncitizen voting is extremely rare across the country, including in Pennsylvania.\n\n## Pennsylvania does not have “early voting” the way many other states do. Instead,  registered voters in Pennsylvania can vote before Election Day only by completing and returning their mail ballot.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nPennsylvanians can vote by mail ballot in person at their county elections office or designated satellite office once ballots have been finalized and are available.\n\nIn Pennsylvania, registered voters can vote before Election Day only by completing and returning their mail-in ballot. Although voters can return their mail ballots up to and including Election Day, they had to apply for their mail ballot by 5 p.m. one week before Election Day.\n\n### \n\nVoters have three ways to request their mail-in ballot:\n\n* online,\n* by mail, or\n* in person at their county election office (or other officially designated satellite office). With this option, a voter can request, receive, complete, and return their mail ballot all in one visit.\n\nAlthough voters can return their completed ballot before Election Day, state law does not allow election officials to open those ballots and prepare them for counting – a process known as pre-canvassing – until 7 a.m. on Election Day. In addition, there are no polling places open for in-person voting before Election Day.\n\nTherefore, Pennsylvania does not ever have “early voting” tallies to report. Results will begin being reported on our Election Night Returns website after the polls close at 8 p.m. on Election Day.\n\nCounty elections offices must begin processing mail ballot applications no later than 50 days before Election Day. That date is not a hard-and-fast deadline for when counties must have ballots available to provide to voters who request them.\n\n## Poll watchers and poll workers fulfill entirely separate roles on Election Day.\n\n## Poll workers are nonpartisan staff who run polling places on Election Day. They check in voters, assist with equipment, track turnout, and may deliver ballot materials after polls close. They cannot engage in partisan political activity. They may also be called election judges, inspectors, or clerks, or the district election board.\n\n## Poll watchers are credentialed representatives of a candidate or political party. They are permitted to observe inside the polling place but cannot interfere with voting.\n\n## Detailed information on what poll watchers can and cannot do is available on the Department’s Guidance Concerning Poll Watchers and Authorized Representatives.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nExcept for voters in the process of voting their own ballot, only duly elected or appointed poll workers are allowed to handle ballots at the polling place. These poll workers swear an oath to \"impartially and faithfully perform [their] duties\" prior to serving in their role. Poll workers are prohibited from engaging in partisan political activity on Election Day.\n\nThose who step up to serve in these important poll worker roles know firsthand that the process of voting in Pennsylvania is free, fair, safe, and secure. Pennsylvania voters can have confidence that their ballot will not be manipulated by those with partisan interests.\n\nPoll watchers, on the other hand, are appointed by candidates appearing on the ballot. A poll watcher must:\n\n* be a qualified registered elector of the county where their appointed polling place is located;\n\n* be identified and receive official county credentials in advance\n\n### \n\n**Poll workers** are elected or appointed to their position and manage the polling place on Election Day. They help keep track of the number of voters,  and ensure the ballot returns are delivered to the county election office after polls close. Poll workers swear an oath to “impartially and faithfully perform [their] duties.”\n\n**Poll watchers** are credentialed representatives of a candidate or political party who may be present inside the polling place during Election Day. They can make good-faith challenges to a voter’s identity or continued residence in the election district. Such challenges must be lodged directly with the Judge of Elections, and poll watchers have no authority to approach voters at the polling place or prevent a voter from voting.\n\nIn addition, poll watchers are not permitted to mark or in any way alter official election records, access the contents of ballot boxes, or interfere with the orderly process of voting.\n\n The Election Code does not permit poll watchers and authorized representatives to handle ballots.  To learn more about poll watchers’ roles, see our Guidance Concerning Poll Watchers and Authorized Representatives page.\n\n## **State and federal laws strictly limit military and law enforcement presence at polling places. Local election officials and the constables they work with will ensure a smooth and secure voting process on election day.**\n\n**The short answer**\n\nThe Pennsylvania Constitution prohibits the military from infringing on the right to vote in an election, and the Pennsylvania Election Code prohibits police officers from being within 100 feet of a polling place — unless they are voting, or serving a warrant, or preventing disturbances at the polling place that interfere with voting. Local peace officers, called constables, are allowed at polling places – they may be uniformed but are different from federal, state, and local law enforcement.  They work with your local election officials to keep the voting process free from disruption.\n\n### \n\nFederal law includes similar protections and prohibits both voter intimidation and the deployment of military personnel to polling places.\n\nThe Department of State expects everyone working in, voting in, or observing polling places to follow the law and remains in frequent contact with county election officials and our law enforcement partners, including the Pennsylvania State Police.\n\nVoters should rest assured that their state and local election officials will not tolerate voter intimidation or election interference of any kind.\n\nLearn more about the rules at the polls.\n\n## **Pennsylvanians won’t always know the final results of all races on election night, and any changes in results that occur after election night are not evidence that an election is “rigged.”**\n\n**The short answer**\n\nHundreds of thousands – sometimes millions – of mail ballots are cast in every election, and **current state law does not permit counties to begin opening these ballots until 7 a.m. on Election Day. That means county election officials cannot even remove the ballots from the envelopes and prepare them to be scanned until that time** – on a day when those same officials are also running more than 9,000 polling places across the state.\n\nThen, under the Election Code, counties may not even begin to record and publish mail ballot results until after the polls close at 8 p.m. Election Day.\n\n### \n\nWhile we would all like to go to bed on election night knowing who won in every race, determining unofficial results is a days-long process because county elections officials must follow the careful, deliberative process and timeline prescribed by Pennsylvania’s Election Code to achieve a thorough count of every eligible vote.\n\nAn accurate count of all votes cast by eligible voters is paramount to our democracy and cannot be rushed. County election workers must be given a reasonable amount of time to do their jobs and follow the law.\n\nEven after the initial rounds of unofficial vote tallies are recorded and published, county canvass boards must continue the elections work. Those boards must meet no later than 9 a.m. on the Friday after the election and must continue canvassing and counting through the eighth day after the election.\n\nAmong other work, these boards adjudicate provisional ballots — ballots that are cast when it’s unclear if the voter is in the correct polling place or when a voter who applied for a mail ballot decides to vote in person instead but doesn’t have their mail ballot to surrender.\n\nIt’s important to note that at every step — during in-person voting at the polls, pre-canvassing and canvassing of mail ballots, and adjudication of provisional ballots — representatives selected by each candidate and political party may be present and observe the process to ensure the integrity of the vote count.\n\nWhile news media “call” races on election night when there appears to be a wide and seemingly insurmountable margin between candidates, it’s important to note that they are basing their calls on unofficial results.\n\nIn close races, every vote must be counted before the outcome can be known.\n\n## States administer elections - not the federal government.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nThe U.S. Constitution gives States the primary authority over election administration. Here in Pennsylvania, the Department of State works with our county partners to administer fair, free, safe, and secure elections.\n\n### \n\nArticle 1, Section 4 of the U.S. Constitution gives States the primary authority over election administration, including the \"times, places, and manner of” federal elections. In Pennsylvania, the General Assembly has passed laws governing our elections, which the Department of State and county partners administer to ensure fair, free, and secure elections. Congress also has the power to prescribe rules governing the “times, places, and manner” of federal elections.\n\nThe President, however, does not have any constitutional authority to decide the rules of elections or administer elections. For that reason, Governor Shapiro has filed a lawsuit challenging President Donald Trump’s signing of Executive Order 14399, an unconstitutional attack on states’ rights and the right to vote for all Pennsylvanians.\n\nThe lawsuit, led by California, Massachusetts, Nevada, and Washington, and joined by Governor Shapiro and 17 other states, challenges the Trump Administration’s unlawful attempt to usurp states’ powers to administer elections. The U.S. Constitution makes clear that elections are to be run by the states.\n\n## **Sometimes voters may receive letters, forms, and applications from organizations related to their voter registration status or applying to vote.  If a voter is uncertain about their registration status, they should go to vote.pa.gov/status.**\n\n## **If they are uncertain about their mail ballot status, they should go to vote.pa.gov/mailballotstatus. You should always be vigilant in reviewing such communications to make sure they come from an official government office.**\n\n**The short answer**\n\nVoter registration information is a public record. Political groups and other non-governmental organizations often use this information to send targeted mail to registered voters, especially during federal election cycles. These mailers may include applications for voter registration or applications for mail ballots that contain pre-filled information such as a voter's name and address. You should make sure the pre-filled information is accurate; if it’s not, your voter record may need to be updated. You can check by going to vote.pa.gov/status.\n\n### \n\nPennsylvania law does not prohibit third parties from sending voter registration applications, mail ballot applications, or other forms related to voting and elections. Completing these applications is optional, and you can always access official Department of State applications.\n\nYou may receive multiple applications from different groups, all asking that you submit the application to your county. You only need to apply one time to register to vote or to receive a mail ballot. If you do submit multiple applications, your county will only process and approve a single registration or one mail ballot. Before filling out a second form, check to see if your county has received an application from you already by going to vote.pa.gov/mailballotstatus.\n\nVoters should also be aware of possible phishing or smishing scams this campaign season . Voters should not click on links in an email or text unless they are certain that the message is from a credible source, and they should never provide personal identifying information in response to such a message.\n\nFor voters seeking accurate information on their registration status, the Department maintains a lookup tool at vote.pa.gov/status. It also maintains a tracker for mail ballot applications and status at vote.pa.gov/mailballotstatus.\n\n## Pennsylvania law requires that candidates’ names on general election ballots appear in a defined political party order – specifically, candidates in the same party as the current governor must be listed first.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nAccording to the Pennsylvania Election Code [25. P.S. § 2963(b)], candidates on general election ballots must be listed based on which party received the most votes in the last gubernatorial election. This means that, if the sitting governor is a Democrat, Democratic candidates in each race must be listed first; and if the sitting governor is a Republican, Republican candidates in each race must be listed first.\n\n### \n\nSince state law dictates the sequence of candidate names on the ballot, county and local officials cannot make any changes to the sequence based on personal preference.\n\nThe required order of candidate names remains in effect for four years, until Pennsylvanians again vote for governor.\n\n## Archived Fact-Checks\n\n### \n\n## Federal intelligence agencies determined a widely shared video posted to social media in late October 2024 that reportedly showed mail ballots in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, being ripped up was a fake that was manufactured by Russian operatives seeking to undermine confidence in the November election.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nOn Oct. 25, 2024, three federal agencies released this statement, which included the following: “This Russian activity is part of Moscow’s broader effort to raise unfounded questions about the integrity of the US election and stoke divisions among Americans … In the lead up to election day and in the weeks and months after, the [intelligence community] expects Russia to create and release additional media content that seeks to undermine trust in the integrity of the election and divide Americans.”.\n\n## Pennsylvania did NOT register hundreds of thousands of new voters without photo ID in the first three months of 2024.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nThe Help America Vote Verification (HAVV) data cited in a 2024 viral social media post does NOT represent the number of newly registered voters in Pennsylvania, and any claim to the contrary is false.\n\nThat HAVV data represents the total number of transactions that Pennsylvania’s 67 counties sent for Social Security number (SSN) verification for multiple purposes, not only for voter registration.\n\n### \n\nThe Pennsylvania Department of State uses HAVV to check partial SSNs not only for voter registration applications, but also for absentee and mail ballot applications. In many cases, a voter’s partial SSN is checked multiple times in a single year.\n\nAs of April 3, 2024, there were approximately 75,000 new voter registrations in 2024. That correct number is publicly available through our website.\n\nFor additional context, voter registration activity historically increases in presidential election years, such as 2024, when compared with other election cycles. For comparison, there were approximately 100,000 new voter registrations during the same period in 2020, and nearly 150,000 new registrations during that period in 2016. \n\n## **Some Pennsylvania counties received large batches of voter registration applications at the voter registration deadline. This is not an uncommon or unlawful practice by organizations conducting voter registration drives and individuals can be sure counties will diligently review each application before it is accepted.**\n\n**The short answer**\n\nIt is not uncommon for organizations conducting voter registration drives to hold on to completed applications and submit them to the county elections office all at once. Although the Department of State discourages this practice because of the administrative burden it may pose, the law does not prohibit it.\n\n### \n\nGrassroots organizers, political organizations, and partisan campaigns play an important role in registering eligible Pennsylvanians to vote and motivating turnout in each election. Whether someone drops off one registration application to a county elections office or one thousand, staff will thoroughly review each application, conduct a HAVA identity check, compare it against existing records to detect duplicates, and verify the applicant’s address. Voters are notified if their registration application is rejected and are given the opportunity to provide additional information.\n\nIf an application or group of applications is suspicious or potentially fraudulent, the county should refer them to the appropriate law enforcement authorities. Voter fraud is extremely rare, and multiple safeguards exist to prevent ineligible individuals from registering or casting a ballot.\n\n## Unsolicited voter registration applications are not being sent by the Pennsylvania Department of State or any county election office in Pennsylvania. Voters are not required to respond to any voter registration or mail ballot application. If a voter is uncertain about their registration status, they should go to vote.pa.gov/status. If they are uncertain about their mail ballot status, they should go to vote.pa.gov/mailballotstatus.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nVoter registration information is a public record. Political groups and other non-governmental organizations often use this information to send targeted mail to registered voters, especially during presidential election cycles. These mailers may include applications for voter registration or applications for mail-in ballots that contain pre-filled information such as a voter's name and address. You should make sure the pre-filled information is accurate; if it’s not, your voter record may need to be updated. You can check by going to vote.pa.gov/status.\n\n### \n\nPennsylvania law does not prohibit third parties from sending voter registration or mail ballot applications. Completing these applications is optional, and you can always access official Department of State applications.\n\nYou may receive multiple applications from different groups, all asking that you submit the application to your county. You only need to apply one time to register to vote or to receive a mail ballot. If you do submit multiple applications, your county will only process and approve a **single registration** or **one mail ballot**. Although your county will keep our elections secure by making sure each voter receives one ballot, duplicate forms can pose a burden on them. Before filling out a second form, you can check to see if your county has received an application from you already by going to vote.pa.gov/mailballotstatus.\n\nVoters should also be aware of possible phishing or smishing scams this campaign season. Texts from the Department provide reminders of upcoming election deadlines and always include our vote.pa.gov url. Voters should not click on links in an email or text unless they are certain that the message is from a credible source, and they should never provide personal identifying information in response to such a message.\n\nVoters can visit the Attorney General’s website to learn more about how they can protect themselves from smishing scams and how they can report them. You can also find some helpful information about avoiding social engineering and phishing attacks on CISA’s website.\n\nFor voters seeking accurate information on their registration status, the Department maintains a lookup tool at vote.pa.gov/status. It also maintains a tracker for mail ballot applications and status at vote.pa.gov/mailballotstatus.\n\n## All voting systems used in Pennsylvania counties are able to accurately read ballots where voters use Sharpie markers to fill in the ovals beside their selected candidates.\n\n**The short answer**\n\nAll claims made since 2020 that Pennsylvania voters’ ballots marked with Sharpies were unable to be counted--as well as claims that election workers deliberately handed out Sharpies to voters in an effort to invalidate their ballots--were determined by the Department of State to be unfounded.\n\n### \n\nCounties may choose to purchase and use only voting systems certified at the federal and state levels. That certification process includes identifying and testing marking devices (pens, pencils, markers, etc.) to ensure the voting system can read them and they do not cause bleed-through or other marking issues. Counties then use that information to identify the best marking devices for their voters. \n\nAll claims made in 2020 and 2022 that Pennsylvania voters’ ballots marked with Sharpies were unable to be counted – as well as claims that election workers were deliberately handing out Sharpies to voters in an effort to invalidate their ballots – were determined by the Department of State to be unfounded.\n\n## **Claims that there were “more votes than voters” in Pennsylvania in the 2020 general election are false.**\n\n**The short answer**\n\nOfficial public documents from the Department of State show that 6,945,045 registered voters successfully cast a ballot in Pennsylvania’s November 2020 election and that a total of 6,915,283 votes were recorded as cast in the presidential election. These two data points clearly show that there were not “more votes than voters” in the 2020 general election.\n\n### \n\nThe November 2020 election was free, fair, safe, and secure. Claims of systemic voter fraud are devoid of any supporting evidence and have consistently been rejected by judges, government agencies, and election experts across the political spectrum. \n\nOn Nov. 24, 2020, the Pennsylvania Department of State officially certified the final results of the 2020 general election based on the certified results provided by each county as statutorily required.\n\nPennsylvania’s Election Code outlines the process for counties to verify the number of voters and the number of ballots cast in each election. That process, which takes place during the official canvass, involves county boards of elections comparing the number of registered voters against the certificates returned by each election district that show the number of people who voted and the number of ballots cast.\n\nThere is no evidence that any county board of elections failed to complete this post-election requirement or identified a discrepancy during this process that could have impacted the outcome of any election, including the 2020 general election.\n\nTrying to extrapolate numbers from the Statewide Uniform Registry of Electors (SURE) system based on vote history contained in the Full Voter Export (FVE) file is not an accurate way to reconcile votes cast to the number of voters who participated in any individual election.\n\nWhile the SURE system is an important tool for county election officials to record the vote history for every individual registered voter in the Commonwealth, it was never intended to – and should not be used to – crosscheck vote totals for any specific election. Voter records in SURE are constantly updated as new voters register, or registered voters move to a different county and have their record transferred, or voters have their record cancelled as a result of moving to a different state, death, or other cancellations pursuant to state and federal law, such as protracted inactivity.\n\nSo, for example, a registered voter in Philadelphia County who voted in the November 2020 general election but moved to New Jersey the next day would have their voter record cancelled in the SURE system when Philadelphia’s Board of Elections receives confirmation that the voter has moved. In that case, the voter’s general information – and the record of their having voted in November 2020 – would no longer be listed in subsequent FVE files.\n\nThe FVE is a snapshot of the moment in time it is downloaded and cannot, therefore, be used to create a reconciliation of votes cast in any individual election.\n\nAdditionally, the SURE system does not publicly display records for all types of voters, including federal voters and confidential voters. Confidential voters are those whose identities are required by law to be kept out of publicly available voter registration databases and lists, and they include victims of domestic violence, stalking, or human trafficking.\n\nFor these reasons, among others, FVE files exported from SURE cannot be used to reconcile vote histories from individual voter files with the number of voters who voted in a given election.\n\nAs we shared with the public in our 2020 General Election Report on our website, a total of 6,945,045 registered voters successfully cast a ballot in Pennsylvania’s Nov. 3, 2020, general election.\n\nAs Pennsylvania’s official certificate of ascertainment – which was signed by Gov. Wolf and is electronically stored within the National Archives – shows, a total of 6,915,283 votes were cast in that year’s presidential race. The certificate breaks down the votes per presidential candidate this way:\n\n* Biden/Harris: 3,458,229\n* Trump/Pence: 3,377,674\n* Jorgensen/Cohen: 79,380\n\nComparing these two official public data sources shows that 29,762 Pennsylvania voters (0.004% of the 6,945,045 voters who cast a ballot) did not have a vote recorded – for whatever reason – for one of the presidential candidates in that year’s race. This comparison clearly shows that there were not more votes than voters in Pennsylvania’s November 2020 general election.\n\n## Related Services and Information\n\nFind more services and information about voting in Pennsylvania.","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":731.2172319998353,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}